Send the control transfer status stage in the direction the host expects.

Windows could see the CDC-ACM device -- it enumerated and created COM5 --
but every attempt to open that port timed out with Win32 error 121,
"the semaphore timeout period has expired".  The reason was the status
stage of a control transfer, not the serial data itself.

A control transfer is SETUP, an optional data stage, and a status stage
(USB 2.0 8.5.3).  The status stage always runs in the direction opposite
to the data stage: after an IN data stage the host sends the zero length
packet, after an OUT data stage the device does.  A transfer with no data
stage at all is always followed by a device-to-host status.

0101 took the direction of the status stage from the endpoint number of
the "control status" event, which is not what the controller reports
there.  Linux does not do it that way either -- in drivers/usb/dwc3/ep0.c
the direction is computed as

    direction = !dwc->ep0_expect_in;

and the status TRB is then submitted on dwc->eps[direction], i.e. on
endpoint 1 for an IN status and endpoint 0 for an OUT status.  ep0_expect_in
is set from the direction bit of the setup packet, so the two agree.

The three cases, and what the host does with each:

  SETUP only (SET_CONFIGURATION, SET_INTERFACE)
      no data stage, the host waits for an IN status.
  IN data (GET_DESCRIPTOR, GET_LINE_CODING)
      the host waits for an OUT status.
  OUT data (SET_LINE_CODING)
      the host waits for an IN status.

Windows sends SET_LINE_CODING with seven payload bytes when it opens the
port, which is the third case: the device has to answer with an IN status.
0101 used the event's endpoint number, which for that transfer is 0, so
the device queued an OUT status instead.  The host waited for a status
that would never come and gave up.

The direction is now derived from the data stage instead of from the
event, so the two working cases keep the direction they had and only the
broken one changes.

--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_usb.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_usb.c
@@ -226,6 +226,7 @@
 	int         sleep_chan;
 	enum { EP0_SETUP, EP0_DATA, EP0_WAIT_STATUS2, EP0_WAIT_STATUS3, EP0_STATUS } state;
 	bool        data_in;        /* data stage direction */
+	bool        status_in;      /* status stage direction: true = IN */
 	uint32_t    events;
 	bool        configured;
 	uint8_t     data_alt;       /* data interface alternate setting */
@@ -486,6 +487,12 @@
 		return;
 	}
 	if (length == 0) {
+		/*
+		 * Two-stage control transfer: SETUP then STATUS.  There is no
+		 * data stage, so the status stage is the device-to-host zero
+		 * length packet (USB 2.0 8.5.3.1).
+		 */
+		ud.status_in = true;
 		ud.state = EP0_WAIT_STATUS2;    /* status stage on XferNotReady */
 		return;
 	}
@@ -767,6 +774,21 @@
 			ud_setup();
 			break;
 		case EP0_DATA:
+			/*
+			 * The status stage runs in the direction opposite to
+			 * the data stage (USB 2.0 8.5.3.1): after an IN data
+			 * stage the host sends the zero length OUT packet,
+			 * after an OUT data stage the device does.
+			 *
+			 * 0101 took the direction from the endpoint number of
+			 * the "control status" event, which is not what the
+			 * controller reports there, so the host was left
+			 * waiting for a status it would never get: Windows
+			 * opens a COM port with SET_LINE_CODING (an OUT data
+			 * stage) and then gave up with Win32 error 121,
+			 * "the semaphore timeout period has expired".
+			 */
+			ud.status_in = !ud.data_in;
 			ud.state = EP0_WAIT_STATUS3;
 			break;
 		case EP0_STATUS:
@@ -780,8 +802,9 @@
 	if (type == 3 && status == 2) { /* XferNotReady, control status phase */
 		if (ud.state == EP0_WAIT_STATUS2 || ud.state == EP0_WAIT_STATUS3) {
 			uint32_t ctl = ud.state == EP0_WAIT_STATUS3 ? TRBCTL_STATUS3 : TRBCTL_STATUS2;
+			unsigned sep = ud.status_in ? 1 : 0;
 			ud.state = EP0_STATUS;
-			ud_start(ep, UD_DATA, 0, ctl);
+			ud_start(sep, UD_DATA, 0, ctl);
 		}
 	}
 }
